Title: "Boolean Set Operations"
Id: "2fe83532-6bca-406e-9a19-94ab03cf93f7"


Text: "The algebra of sets, initiated by George Boole in the mid-nineteenth
       century, exhibits a profound structural isomorphism with propositional
       logic. Every logical connective possesses an exact set-theoretic
       counterpart: disjunction ($\lor$) corresponds to union ($\cup$),
       conjunction ($\land$) corresponds to intersection ($\cap$), and negation
       ($\neg$) corresponds to relative complement or set difference
       ($\setminus$).

       Through these operations, the power set $\mathcal{P}(U)$ of any ambient
       set is endowed with the structure of a complete Boolean algebra,
       providing the computational engine for combinatorics, measure theory,
       topology, and probability."
Id: "fa87c699-877a-4604-8cf0-ba2bb87bbfe8"


[A \.set.union./ B]
Defines: C := \set@{c_ : c_ "in" U | (.c_ "in"? A.) \.or./ (.c_ "in"? B.)}
using: U is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "union of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \cup B?"
. description: "The union $A \cup B$ is the set containing all elements that
                belong to $A$, to $B$, or to both."
Id: "a08ae02b-7a4e-4911-863a-7d929540f193"


[A \.set.intersect./ B]
Defines: C := \set@{c_ : c_ "in" U | (.c_ "in"? A.) \.and./ (.c_ "in"? B.)}
using: U is \set
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "intersection of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \cap B?"
. description: "The intersection $A \cap B$ is the set of elements common to
                both $A$ and $B$, containing only those elements that belong to
                both."
Id: "5d137355-ba4e-44ca-bf58-2f7b3f667dcc"


[A \.set.difference./ B]
Defines: C := \set@{c_ : c_ "in" A | c_ \.not.in./ B}
when: A, B is \set
Documented:
. called: "set difference of $A?$ and $B?$"
. written: "A? \setminus B?"
. description: "The set difference (relative complement) $A \setminus B$ is the
                set of elements that belong to $A$ but do not belong to $B$."
Id: "186de027-9bf6-422d-a2ed-823c94a80b1b"


Text: "The power set constructor $\mathcal{P}(A)$ collects all subsets of $A$
       into a single universe of discourse. It constitutes the carrier set for
       the Boolean algebra associated with $A$."
Id: "e3164791-b17c-427a-8c8c-02ec40801629"


[\power.set:of{A}]
Defines: P := \set@{X_ : X_ \:subset:/ A}
when: A is \set
Documented:
. called: "power set of $A?$"
. written: "\mathcal{P}(A?)"
. description: "The power set $\mathcal{P}(A)$ is the set of all subsets of $A$."
Id: "3c3133ac-1cc2-4c76-8e84-fb332c7b08af"


Text: "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are said to be *disjoint* (or mutually exclusive)
       when they share no members in common, so that their intersection is
       empty: $A \cap B = \emptyset$. Disjointness is the set-theoretic
       formulation of incompatibility."
Id: "185d9e5d-1d5e-4478-863e-792c9889a309"


[A \.disjoint.from./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that: (.A \.set.intersect./ B.) = \empty.set
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is disjoint from $B?$"
. description: "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are disjoint if their intersection is
                empty: $A \cap B = \emptyset$."
Id: "3607e1a0-1e2c-40e7-93f1-728cdec9968e"


SectionTitle: "Algebraic Properties of Set Operations"
Id: "e8c4cfac-dbb8-4e62-a5c1-20dfcd50799e"


Text: "Set union and intersection satisfy algebraic symmetries that mirror the
       algebra of real numbers, including commutativity, associativity, and
       distributivity. However, Boolean algebras exhibit unique dualities—such
       as the mutual distributivity of union over intersection and intersection
       over union—that have no analogue in numerical arithmetic."
Id: "aebe6956-53f1-4b24-af92-63732dfe1544"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then:
. (.A \.set.union./ B.) = (.B \.set.union./ A.)
. (.A \.set.intersect./ B.) = (.B \.set.intersect./ A.)
Documented:
. called: "Commutativity of Set Operations"
. description: "Union and intersection are commutative: $A \cup B = B \cup A$
                and $A \cap B = B \cap A$."
Proof: """By definition, $x \in A \cup B \iff x \in A \lor x \in B$.
          Because logical disjunction is commutative,
          $x \in A \lor x \in B \iff x \in B \lor x \in A \iff x \in B \cup A$.
          By Extensionality, $A \cup B = B \cup A$.
          Similarly, $x \in A \cap B \iff x \in A \land x \in B \iff x \in B \land x \in A \iff x \in B \cap A$,
          so $A \cap B = B \cap A$."""
Id: "a8b70f59-148f-4e51-bcc8-7ab889db86d4"


Text: "In the monoid of subsets under union, the empty set $\emptyset$ serves as
       the unique neutral identity element: adjoining nothing leaves any set
       unchanged."
Id: "20e77baa-c3d2-4104-a8dc-3fe1e02717e8"


Theorem:
given: A is \set
then: (.A \.set.union./ \empty.set.) = A
Documented:
. called: "Empty Set Union Identity"
. description: "Union of any set $A$ with the empty set yields $A$:
                $A \cup \emptyset = A$."
Proof: """An element $x$ belongs to $A \cup \emptyset$ if and only if
          $x \in A \lor x \in \emptyset$. Since $\emptyset$ has no members, the
          predicate $x \in \emptyset$ is identically false for all $x$. Thus
          $x \in A \lor \text{false} \iff x \in A$. By Extensionality,
          $A \cup \emptyset = A$."""
Id: "e0a17409-7d0a-4286-9a25-9c8eb00a40df"


Theorem:
given:
. U, A, B, C is \set
where:
. A \:subset?:/ U
. B \:subset?:/ U
. C \:subset?:/ U
then:
. (.(.A \.set.union./ B.) \.set.union./ C.) = (.A \.set.union./ (.B \.set.union./ C.).)
. (.(.A \.set.intersect./ B.) \.set.intersect./ C.) = (.A \.set.intersect./ (.B \.set.intersect./ C.).)
. (.A \.set.union./ B.) = (.B \.set.union./ A.)
. (.A \.set.intersect./ B.) = (.B \.set.intersect./ A.)
. (.A \.set.union./ A.) = A
. (.A \.set.intersect./ A.) = A
. (.A \.set.intersect./ (.B \.set.union./ C.).) = (.(.A \.set.intersect./ B.) \.set.union./ (.A \.set.intersect./ C.).)
. (.A \.set.union./ (.B \.set.intersect./ C.).) = (.(.A \.set.union./ B.) \.set.intersect./ (.A \.set.union./ C.).)
. (.U \.set.difference./ (.A \.set.union./ B.).) = (.(.U \.set.difference./ A.) \.set.intersect./ (.U \.set.difference./ B.).)
. (.U \.set.difference./ (.A \.set.intersect./ B.).) = (.(.U \.set.difference./ A.) \.set.union./ (.U \.set.difference./ B.).)
Documented:
. called: "Boolean Laws for Subsets"
. description: "Relative to a fixed universe, union and intersection are
                associative, commutative, idempotent, mutually distributive, and
                satisfy De Morgan's laws for relative complements."
Proof: """Each equality follows by converting the set membership condition
          into the corresponding propositional equivalence and invoking the
          Axiom of Extensionality. For De Morgan's first law,
          $x \in U \setminus (A \cup B) \iff x \in U \land \neg(x \in A \lor x \in B)$.
          Applying propositional De Morgan yields
          $x \in U \land (\neg(x \in A) \land \neg(x \in B)) \iff (x \in U \land x \notin A) \land (x \in U \land x \notin B)$,
          which is precisely the definition of $x \in (U \setminus A) \cap (U \setminus B)$.
          The dual laws follow symmetrically."""
Id: "aa8bf616-5e94-4976-a63a-132732577422"


[\indexed.union:of{A}:over{I}:in{U}]
Defines: B \:subset:/ U
when:
. I, U is \set
. A is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
expresses:
. forAll: x "in" U
  then:
  . have: x "in"? B
    iff:
    . exists:
      . i "in" I
      . S is \set
      suchThat:
      . S = A(i)
      . x "in"? S
Documented:
. called: "union of the family $A?$ over $I?$"
. written: "\bigcup_{i?\in I?} A?_{i?}"
. description: "An element belongs to the indexed union exactly when it belongs
                to at least one member of the family."
Id: "b256bfcd-6772-4a1f-9609-210bae9d3b11"


[\indexed.intersection:of{A}:over{I}:in{U}]
Defines: B \:subset:/ U
when:
. I, U is \set
. A is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
expresses:
. forAll: x "in" U
  then:
  . have: x "in"? B
    iff:
    . forAll: i "in" I
      then:
      . exists: S is \set
        suchThat:
        . S = A(i)
        . x "in"? S
Documented:
. called: "intersection of the family $A?$ over $I?$ in $U?$"
. written: "\bigcap_{i?\in I?} A?_{i?}"
. description: "An element of the ambient universe belongs to the indexed
                intersection exactly when it belongs to every member of the
                family. Naming $U$ makes the empty intersection unambiguous."
Id: "3afa1a1b-3d36-437f-baca-56a07eae43c0"


Theorem:
given:
. I, U is \set
. A(i__) is \function:on{I}:to{\power.set:of{U}}
where: I = \empty.set
then:
. \indexed.union:of{A}:over{I}:in{U} = \empty.set
. \indexed.intersection:of{A}:over{I}:in{U} = U
Documented:
. called: "Empty Indexed Union and Intersection"
. description: "The union of an empty family is empty, while its intersection is
                the explicitly supplied ambient universe."
Proof: """For the indexed union, $x \in \bigcup_{i \in \emptyset} A_i$ requires
          the existence of an index $i \in \emptyset$ such that $x \in A_i$.
          Because $\emptyset$ contains no indices, this existential condition is
          unconditionally false for every $x \in U$, yielding the empty set
          $\emptyset$. For the indexed intersection,
          $x \in \bigcap_{i \in \emptyset} A_i$ requires that for all
          $i \in \emptyset$, $x \in A_i$. Because the antecedent
          $i \in \emptyset$ is false, the conditional implication is vacuously
          true for every element $x \in U$. Consequently, every element of the
          ambient universe $U$ satisfies the definition, yielding $U$."""
Id: "7ef6b89a-1cc1-46f3-9537-3bacf672a499"
